The undo pass rolls back all losers together in **one backward sweep** of the log. Each loser has a next record to undo (its lastLSN, or a CLR's UndoNextLSN). At every step ARIES picks the **largest** of those LSNs, processes that record, and repeats until no loser is left (paper Fig. 12):

- an update: undo it on the page, write a CLR, set the page's pageLSN to the CLR's LSN, move to the record's PrevLSN;
- a CLR: just jump to its UndoNextLSN;
- when a transaction's chain reaches 0, write its TXN-END.

Undo does not consult the DPT or compare pageLSNs: thanks to redo, every logged change is known to be on the page.
